{"id": "73a1948b1f0b9c8e", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-30T02:27:29.817181+00:00", "author": "high3", "kind": "verification", "claim": "Let (G) be a finite connected undirected graph with at least one edge. Suppose every vertex of (G) has even degree. Prove that (G) has a closed trail that traverses every edge exactly once.", "evidence": "verdict: correct", "verifiable": false, "status": "open", "fact_id": "f6bdc8c9ef1a7c62", "links": {"source_id": "56f5bd8e5aee45c8", "predecessors": []}, "glossary": {}, "verdict": "correct", "write_error": null, "verification_report": {"summary": "The proof is correct. It constructs a maximum-length nonempty trail using finiteness and the existence of an edge; proves that trail is closed by an incidence-parity argument at its terminal vertex, correctly counting loops twice; and then uses connectedness to find an unused edge incident with the trail if any edge were omitted. Cyclically rotating the closed trail and appending that edge contradicts maximality. Every hypothesis is appropriately used, and there are no external references or prohibited proof patterns.", "critical_errors": [], "gaps": []}}
{"id": "c446c4b1f2c94832", "timestamp_utc": "2026-08-30T02:27:38.952172+00:00", "author": "high4", "kind": "verification", "claim": "Let (G) be a finite connected undirected graph with at least one edge. Suppose every vertex of (G) has even degree. Prove that (G) has a closed trail that traverses every edge exactly once.", "evidence": "verdict: correct", "verifiable": false, "status": "open", "fact_id": "b14a2c45d5387c3f", "links": {"source_id": "7d1ceda8c04d68e4", "predecessors": []}, "glossary": {}, "verdict": "correct", "write_error": null, "verification_report": {"summary": "The proof is correct and complete. It chooses a maximum-length trail, uses the even-degree hypothesis to show that the trail must be closed, and then uses connectedness, cyclic rotation of the closed trail, and maximality to show that no edge can be omitted. Every hypothesis is used, the reasoning remains valid when loops are allowed, and there are no prohibited citations, unproven conditional premises, vague external appeals, reference defects, or missing justifications.", "critical_errors": [], "gaps": []}}

